\section{Data and Methods}

This analysis uses single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 study (3CA:20773) \url{https://doi.org/10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8}, comprising 58,843 cells and 33,538 genes from 10 meningioma samples. User-supplied metabolic genes (1,988 symbols in \texttt{inputs/metabolic_genes_total.csv}) were matched to the expression matrix via exact-symbol aggregation, yielding 1,646 genes after prevalence filtering.

\subsection{Normalization and Feature Scaling}

Expression values were normalized using per-cell library size scaling to 10,000 UMI counts, followed by log1p transformation:
\begin{equation}
x'_{gi} = \log\left(1 + \frac{10^4 \cdot x_{gi}}{\sum_{h} x_{hi}}\right)
\end{equation}
where $x_{gi}$ is the raw UMI count for gene $g$ in cell $i$, and the denominator is the total UMI count across all genes in cell $i$. Features were then scaled using Scanpy's \texttt{scale} function with \texttt{zero\_center=False} and \texttt{max\_value=10}.

\subsection{Clustering and Baselines}

Leiden clustering was performed on the first 30 PCA components (n\_neighbors=15) at resolution 0.8, selected as the highest median silhouette across five random seeds [0, 17, 42, 73, 101]. Stability was assessed via pairwise ARI across seeds (mean=0.965, min=0.947). A matched-k KMeans control (k=17, seed=0) yielded silhouette=0.220. Nineteen random-gene controls (size-matched from non-metabolic, non-mitochondrial symbols) produced silhouettes ranging from 0.199 to 0.222, with only 2 of 19 (add-one rank fraction=0.15) achieving silhouette $\ge$ the metabolic control.

\section{Question 1: Do all cells form distinct, reproducible candidate transcriptional metabolic clusters?}

\textbf{Answer: Candidate partitions observed but distinct metabolic states inconclusive.}

Global analysis of 58,843 cells using 1,646 matched metabolic genes yielded 17 Leiden clusters at resolution 0.8. Cluster sizes ranged from 714 to 8,806 cells (median $\approx$ 3,500). Leiden silhouette=0.217, matched-k KMeans silhouette=0.220, and mean pairwise ARI=0.965 across seeds.

Random-gene controls (19 total) produced silhouettes with mean=0.210, min=0.199, max=0.222. Only 2 controls exceeded or equaled the metabolic control (add-one rank fraction=0.15). The HVG baseline yielded silhouette=0.283, exceeding the metabolic control (0.220).

\textbf{Interpretation:} The observed silhouette (0.217) is comparable to random controls (mean=0.210), and the add-one rank fraction (0.15) indicates that metabolic genes do not systematically outperform random gene sets. The HVG baseline (0.283) is stronger, suggesting that the 
